    ],
    "justification": "The exact rational witness makes the cohort-mean PPML topology loss quantitatively auditable in the smallest cyclic staggered panel.",
    "gap": "Published multiplicative DiD examples do not report this PTT-specific canonical bound and exact cycle loss for a legal nonnegative covariance law.",
    "consumer": "The exhibit provides a reproducible calibration for implementations of the cycle correction.",
    "proof_tex": "Use the untreated--treated stacking\n\\[\nU=((2,1),(3,1),(3,2),(\\infty,1),(\\infty,2),(\\infty,3)),\n\\qquad\nD=((2,2),(2,3),(3,3)).                                                    \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe smallest observed mean in \\(\\mathsf E_3\\) is one.  Cohorts \\(2\\) and \\(\\infty\\) have coordinatewise noise magnitude \\(1/10\\), while each coordinate of cohort \\(3\\)'s noise has magnitude at most\n\\(1/10+1/20=3/20\\).  Hence every realized outcome is at least\n\\(1-3/20=17/20>0\\).\n\nThe treated observed and counterfactual totals are\n\\[\nA=\\frac13\\left(3+\\frac92+\\frac92\\right)=4,\n\\qquad\nB=\\frac13(2+3+3)=\\frac83,\n\\qquad\n\\theta_{\\mathrm{PTT}}=\\frac{A}{B}-1=\\frac12.                           \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThere are six untreated edges and six vertices in the connected untreated graph, so\n\\(q=6-6+1=1\\).  The unique fundamental-cycle column obtained from its four signed mean reciprocals is\n\\[\nR=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n0&1&-1/2&-1&1/2&0&0&0&0\n\\end{pmatrix}^{\\prime}.                                                  \\tag{3}\n\\]\nFor auditability, with parameter order\n\\((\\log a_2,\\log a_3,\\log a_\\infty,\\log b_2,\\log b_3)\\), the treated-counterfactual derivative and target gradient are\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial B}{\\partial\\beta}\n=\\begin{pmatrix}5/3&1&0&2/3&2\\end{pmatrix}^{\\prime},\n\\qquad\nc_\\beta=-\\frac9{16}\\frac{\\partial B}{\\partial\\beta},\n\\qquad\nc_D=\\begin{pmatrix}1/8&1/8&1/8\\end{pmatrix}^{\\prime}.                  \\tag{4}\n\\]\nSolving \\(S w=c_\\beta\\) and using\n\\(h_{\\mathrm{CM},U}=P_UXw\\) gives\n\\[\nh_{\\mathrm{CM}}=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n-15/16&3/16&-3/8&3/4&3/16&-3/8&1/8&1/8&1/8\n\\end{pmatrix}^{\\prime}.                                                  \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe covariance matrices are\n\\[\n\\Sigma_2=\\Sigma_\\infty=\\frac1{100}I_3,\n\\qquad\n\\Sigma_3=\\frac1{400}I_3+vv^{\\prime},\n\\qquad\nv=\\begin{pmatrix}1/10&-1/10&1/10\\end{pmatrix}^{\\prime}.                \\tag{6}\n\\]\nThe eigenvalues of \\(\\Sigma_3\\) are\n\\(1/400,1/400,13/400\\), so covariance interior holds.  With\n\\[\n\\Omega=\\Pi\\operatorname{blockdiag}(3\\Sigma_2,3\\Sigma_3,3\\Sigma_\\infty)\n\\Pi^{\\prime},                                                           \\tag{7}\n\\]\nsubstitution of (3), (5), and (6) gives exactly\n\\[\nb_{\\mathrm{cyc}}=R^{\\prime}\\Omega h_{\\mathrm{CM}}=\\frac9{640},\n\\qquad\nK=R^{\\prime}\\Omega R=\\frac{183}{1600}.                                 \\tag{8}\n\\]\nTherefore \\(b_{\\mathrm{cyc}}/K=15/122\\), and the projection identity gives\n\\[\nh^{\\star}=h_{\\mathrm{CM}}-R\\frac{15}{122}\n=\\begin{pmatrix}\n-15/16&63/976&-153/488&213/244&123/976&-3/8&1/8&1/8&1/8\n\\end{pmatrix}^{\\prime}.                                                  \\tag{9}\n\\]\nMultiplication using (6)--(9) yields\n\\[\nh_{\\mathrm{CM}}^{\\prime}\\Omega h_{\\mathrm{CM}}\n=\\frac{6663}{102400},\n\\qquad\nh^{\\star\\prime}\\Omega h^{\\star}\n=\\frac{395643}{6246400},                                                 \\tag{10}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\Gamma\n=\\frac{b_{\\mathrm{cyc}}^2}{K}\n=\\frac{27}{15616}\n=\\frac{6663}{102400}-\\frac{395643}{6246400}.                            \\tag{11}\n\\]\nThe covariance identity for \\(Z\\) identifies the first number in (10) as\n\\(\\operatorname{Var}(h_{\\mathrm{CM}}^{\\prime}Z)\\), and the canonical-gradient theorem identifies the second as \\(V^{\\star}\\).  Finally,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Gamma}{V^{\\star}}=\\frac{3600}{131881}\\approx0.0272973,\n\\]\nso the cohort-mean PPML variance exceeds the bound by approximately \\(2.73\\) percent of \\(V^{\\star}\\).",
